## What I did

The refusal at src/tools.rs:208 told a confined /spawn worker what it blocked and where the fence is — and nothing it could type instead. Every other refusal I've deliberately hardened (#767, #748, #761, #749) names its hatches verbatim; this one was the odd one out, and it fires exactly where a worker can't ask a human.

**`safety::git_redirection_refusal_message(reason, root, plain)`** — pure, beside the detector:

• First sentence byte-identical, so nothing reading the current text breaks.
• Alternatives branch on the matched class, decided from the detector's own reason string via classify_redirection_reason — not a second matcher over the command.
• Flag class → all three hatches (drop it; point it inside with git -C sub / `git --work-tree=.; hand it to the parent session). Env class → **hatches 1 and 3 only**, because GIT_DIR=/GIT_WORK_TREE=` are refused even when relative. Offering the in-root form there would name an alternative that's *also* refused — the exact Claude Code bug I transferred from.
• Unrecognised reason → conservative env branch rather than a guessed hatch.
• No bypass flag added; the confinement is deliberate.

Verified rather than assumed: I ran the grep myself — 19 mentions, exactly one executable consumer (tools.rs:208), two comments, rest is definition + tests. No second door.

Tests at the emission point, including the one I'd defend hardest: every command the message tells you to type is fed through the real detector and must return None. Prose drifts; that assertion can't. My own glyph test also caught an em dash I'd left in plain mode — so the dash is parameterized, not just the bullet. No existing detector test weakened.

cargo fmt --check, clippy -D warnings, build, test (5041 + 7 suites) all green. Size register updated for the two files I touched, with reasons; CLAUDE.md's now-false "names what matched" claim corrected in the same commit.
